            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"To be set apart for sacred use, and the ongoing theological process by which a believer is made holy, purified from sin, and transformed into the likeness of Christ by the work of the Holy Spirit (progressive sanctification).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"John 17:17\",\"1 Thessalonians 4:3\",\"1 Thessalonians 5:23\",\"Romans 6:19-22\",\"1 Peter 1:2\",\"Philippians 2:12-13\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Definitive or positional sanctification, emphasizing that believers are already perfectly set apart and declared objectively holy in Christ at the moment of salvation, regardless of their ongoing moral progress.\",\"basis\":[\"Hebrews 10:10\",\"1 Corinthians 1:2\",\"1 Corinthians 6:11\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Entire sanctification or Christian perfection (Wesleyan-Holiness tradition), viewed as a distinct second work of grace where a believer is entirely cleansed from original sin and made perfect in love during this present life.\",\"basis\":[\"1 Thessalonians 5:23\",\"1 John 1:9\",\"Matthew 5:48\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Ritual or ceremonial separation, referring to physical objects, places, animals, or days being consecrated for exclusive use by God rather than internal moral transformation of a person.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 2:3\",\"Exodus 29:44\",\"Leviticus 8:10-12\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While there is a strong, settled consensus that sanctification broadly means being 'set apart' or 'made holy,' major Christian traditions differ on the mechanics, timing, and extent of the process (e.g., progressive sanctification in Reformed theology versus entire sanctification in Wesleyan theology).\"}",
